@article{li2020what,
  title = {{What’s New in Hip Replacement}},
  author = {Mengnai Li and Andrew H. Glassman},
  year = {2020},
  journal = {Journal of Bone and Joint Surgery},
  publisher = {Wolters Kluwer},
  volume = {102},
  number = {18},
  pages = {1572--1580},
  doi = {10.2106/jbjs.20.00927},
  url = {https://openalex.org/W2893738728},
  note = {OpenAlex W2893738728; blocks b1+b1v3},
  abstract = {About 19\% of patients with degenerative joint disease awaiting a total hip arthroplasty (THA) experience a health state worse than death. Pain is the main contributing factor1. Reducing the burden of completing complicated patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs) remains a challenge. A possible alternative is the use of a modified Single Assessment Numerical Evaluation (M-SANE). This 1-question PROM was shown to perform similarly to multiple-question PROMs among patients undergoing primary THA2. Implant Design and Related Outcomes Cemented Compared with Cementless Stem Fixation In a recent study on 1,671 Medicare patients undergoing primary THAs during a 4-year span (2014 to 2017), cemented femoral fixation outperformed cementless fixation, with a significantly higher likelihood of discharge home and a trend toward a shorter length of stay and lower readmission rate, cost of care, and reoperation rate3. A Norwegian Arthroplasty Register study based on 66,995 THAs from 2005 to 2017 also found the highest risk of revision in elderly women (≥55 years of age) receiving cementless stems, mainly due to periprosthetic fracture and dislocation4. Highly Cross-Linked Polyethylene (HXLPE) A long-term follow-up (mean, 16 years) of a prospectively collected cohort of 237 patients <50 years of age demonstrated significantly less wear-related revision and higher survivorship in the HXLPE group compared with the non-HXLPE group5. The evidence from the National Joint Registry for England, Wales, Northern Ireland and the Isle of Man (NJR) revision data set indicated that the best survival at a 12-year follow-up was observed in THAs utilizing polyethylene liners subjected to a total radiation dose between ≥5 Mrad and <10 Mrad, followed by remelting, and incorporating an asymmetrical face. An irradiation dose of ≥10 Mrad showed no further risk reduction6. Ceramic-on-Ceramic or Ceramic-on-Polyethylene Design Two long-term follow-up studies (minimum, 15 years) on a ceramic-on-ceramic design and/or ceramic-on-polyethylene design demonstrated no sign of osteolysis based on radiographic or computed tomographic (CT) imaging7,8. The baseline ceramic fracture rate in the contemporary ceramic-on-ceramic design was also investigated. In a Level-I meta-analysis study, Yoon et al. reported a lower rate of ceramic fracture in the delta group (0.2\%) compared with the forte group (0.5\%). Also, the ceramic fracture rate per 1,000 patient-years was 0.5 for the delta group and 0.9 for the forte group. The ceramic head fracture rate was 0.1\% for the delta group compared with 0.2\% for the forte group9. Dual-Mobility Construct A Level-III propensity score matching study from the Nordic Arthroplasty Register Association (NARA) demonstrated that the use of a dual-mobility construct as primary treatment for hip fracture (4,520 hip fractures) was associated with an overall lower risk of revision, especially due to dislocation10. The conclusion of the study ought to be interpreted with caution, as a majority of the THAs were performed via a posterolateral approach and there was an overall higher mortality rate with a dual-mobility construct (40.1\%) than with a conventional cup (26.6\%)10,11. In another study, 100\% survivorship at 10 to 16 years was reported, using a contemporary dual-mobility construct design with hydroxyapatite coating. No dislocations, revisions, or osteolysis were observed. However, there was a very high number of patients lost to follow-up (167 of 310 died before 10 years)12. Reduced rates of dislocation were also found in a dual-mobility construct compared with a conventional bearing in a retrospective case-control study among 267 revision THA cases done via anterior and lateral approaches at an intermediate-term follow-up (24 to 73 months)13. Patient Factors in Relation to Outcomes Not all patients recover at the same rate. A Level-III prognostic study from the Dutch joint registry (6,030 patients) identified 3 subgroups of patients with different functional recovery trajectories after THA: fast starters (87.7\%), slow starters (4.6\%), and late dippers (7.7\%)14. Female patients and patients who smoked tended to be slow starters, and older age (>75 years), obesity, higher American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) grades (III or IV), worse EuroQol-5 Dimensions (EQ-5D) scores, cemented acetabular implants, and a direct lateral approach were risk factors for the late dipper category. An anterior approach was protective against a less favorable response14. Young Patients Excellent clinical outcome scores and survivorship could be expected in patients who were ≤20 years of age at an intermediate-term to long-term follow-up using modern implants15. Race and Ethnicity A prognostic Level-III study of Kaiser Permanente universally insured patients found similar or lower rates of lifetime reoperation and 90-day postoperative events in minority patients compared with white patients, and black and Hispanic patients had higher rates of 90-day emergency department visits16. Medical Comorbidities Medical comorbidities including preoperative malnourishment (albumin, <3.4 g/L), polycythemia vera, preoperative anemia (even as mild as hematocrit of 27\% to 36\%), Parkinson disease, and dementia usually have a negative impact with regard to THA outcomes17-21. However, a history of prostate cancer did not increase the risk of short-term complications after THA22. The in-hospital mortality for patients with Parkinson disease was not increased according to a matched cohort study from the National Inpatient Sample (NIS)19. Patients undergoing dialysis who are on a transplant list should delay an arthroplasty until the transplant has taken place to minimize complications. For patients undergoing total joint arthroplasty (TJA), researchers found a significantly higher rate of postoperative complications (especially surgical site infection and periprosthetic joint infection) among patients undergoing dialysis at 18\% compared with the renal transplant group at 3.5\%. Patients in the dialysis group also had a higher rate of revision at 24\% than patients in the transplant group at 3.5\%. Patients in the dialysis group had lower survivorship than patients in the transplant group23. The 5-year mortality rates following primary THA were 62.6\% for patients undergoing renal dialysis, 37.3\% for patients undergoing a kidney transplant, and 15.0\% for patients with non-renal disease. Patients undergoing dialysis and those undergoing a transplant are high-risk groups, with significantly increased 90-day readmission incidences of 55\% for patients undergoing dialysis and 43\% for patients undergoing a transplant, making it challenging to include them into a bundled payment model24. Patients with a lower estimated glomerular filtration rate (GFR), and especially those with <30 mL/min/1.73 m2, are more likely to sustain medical complications and have a mortality rate 6 to 9 times higher than those with a normal estimated GFR undergoing primary THA25. For those patients who were dialysis-dependent because of end-stage renal disease, the peritoneal dialysis group had a significantly lower risk of periprosthetic joint infection compared with the hemodialysis group26. A prognostic matched case-control study showed that the 15-year cumulative risk of revision was closely related to the etiology of osteonecrosis, ranging from 25\% to 0\% for idiopathic, steroid-induced, posttraumatic, alcohol-induced, and radiation-induced etiologies in descending order27. Prior Hip Arthroscopy In patients with a diagnosis of osteoarthritis who underwent hip arthroscopy, Malahias et al. found unacceptably high rates of conversion to THA (up to 68.4\%) within 2 years. Additionally, following conversion to THA, those patients faced a significantly increased risk of revision within 2 years after the index THA28 (odds ratio [OR], 3.72 [95\% confidence interval (CI), 3.15 to 4.57]; p = 0.012). The conversion group was also found to have increased surgical time and increased perioperative complications29. Prior Femoral Osteotomy There is no clear evidence to support the practice of staged implant removal to mitigate the risk of intraoperative fracture or subsequent infection, although the conversion surgical procedure is technically more demanding and may carry a higher risk of complications30. Body Mass Index (BMI) An Australian meta-analysis and systematic review study of 2,190,824 patients found that obese and morbidly obese patients remained at a higher risk of complications after THA, especially deep infection31. This was supported by a Dutch study of 218,214 THAs that showed that the ASA score and BMI (especially >40 kg/m2) were the strongest predictors for 1-year revision. BMI and previous hip surgical procedure were independent risk factors for revision after 3 years32. Spine Pathology In candidates for THA, flatback deformity (estimated 16\%) and hyperlordosis (estimated 25\%) are quite common33. Although patients with prior spine fusion had a lower self-reported physical activity level compared with patients without spine fusion both before and after THA, both groups saw a similar degree of improvement in physical activity level following THA34. Preoperative Opioid Use Preoperative opioid use continues to be an area of active research. Vakharia et al. demonstrated, in a matched control analysis of 42,097 patients with Medicare, that patients with opioid use disorder were at greater risk to experience implant-related complications, periprosthetic joint infections, and readmissions and had higher costs following primary THA compared with patients without opioid use disorder35. Bonner et al. also reported significantly lower PROMs, longer hospital stays, and a more likely discharge to rehabilitation in this population36. Surgical Factors in Relation to Outcome Surgical Approach In a recent American Association of Hip and Knee Surgeons (AAHKS) online survey, over one-half (56.2\%) of 996 total respondents currently perform the direct anterior approach37. A multicenter, prospective study of 3,018 participants found a dramatic PROM score improvement at 6 months following THA regardless of the surgical approach (posterior, transgluteal, and anterior)38. This was supported by a Mayo Clinic study showing similar 30-day major and minor complications regardless of the surgical approach employed (direct anterior, lateral, and posterior)39. Another multicenter, prospective, international study using propensity score matching found no superior early postoperative PROM scores (1.5 months postoperatively) in patients who underwent a THA performed with a direct anterior approach compared with those who underwent a THA performed with a posterolateral approach. No PROM differences were found at ≥1 year up to 5 years40. A large, retrospective study of 16,186 consecutive THAs investigated the early mechanical complications (within 2 years of the surgical procedure) using 3 different approaches: 5,465 using the direct anterior approach, 8,561 using the direct lateral approach, and 2,160 using the posterolateral approach41. The adjusted risks of instability within 2 years were 0.17\% for the direct lateral approach, 0.74\% for the direct anterior approach, and 1.74\% for the posterolateral approach. Femoral component failure, including fracture or loosening, occurred more frequently for the direct anterior approach (1.20\%) compared with the direct lateral approach (0.58\%) and the posterolateral approach (0.47\%). The direct lateral approach seemed to have the lowest overall risk of mechanical complications41. Another retrospective study of the NJR using propensity score matching reported similar 5-year survivorship free of revision for dislocation between the posterior and anterolateral approaches, and lower risks of mortality and intraoperative complications when treating patients with a femoral neck fracture with a posterolateral approach42. Complications According to a retrospective study of 4,555 primary cementless THAs from a single institution, wound-related complications remained the most common reason for reoperation, followed by periprosthetic fracture and pain; dislocation and acute periprosthetic joint infection accounted for a total of 79\% of revision procedures involving an isolated exchange of at least 1 modular implant (head or liner)43. Venous Thromboembolism and Anticoagulation Approximately 1 in 167 patients undergoing THA develops venous thromboembolism within 30 days after a surgical procedure. The reported rates of venous thromboembolism following THA were stable between 2008 and 201644. Glassberg and Lachiewicz demonstrated an increase usage of rivaroxaban, and a decrease in warfarin use, after elective THA among commercially insured (23,768 patients) and Medicare beneficiaries (12,155 patients) between 2010 and 2015. Although there was a similar risk of bleeding, warfarin users were more likely to experience deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism than rivaroxaban45. Osteolysis and Aseptic Loosening The practice of using radiostereometric analysis (RSA) for monitoring implant migration to predict long-term stability of cementless implants has recently been questioned. Frazer and Tanzer reported only 6 of the initial 9 stems with “anticipated stability” had favorable survivorship at the 10-year follow-up, and 1 of 2 initially poor performing stems according to RSA actually did well at 10 years46. Close surveillance of patients with an Exactech Connexion GXL liner was recommended following the observation of 12 cases of early postoperative failure due to wear and severe osteolysis at a single institution in addition to the 22 cases reported to the Manufacturer and User Facility Device Experience database from 2009 to 2019. A unique “polar-locking” mechanism on the back side of the liner seemed to be the cause47. Dislocation Numerous studies on standard precautions compared with relaxed or no posterior precautions after THA via a posterolateral approach showed no difference in the risk of dislocation48,49. A magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) study of 32 symptomatic patients (postoperative hip pain, locking, or perceived instability) revealed that only 50\% of the surgically repaired posterior remained at 1 year after THA performed via a posterolateral approach. dislocation occurred in 2 of 15 in the group compared with of in the A study found although posterior and were performed during direct anterior approach THA, with was frequently at 1 year of 32 The anterior remained 100\% in the group with intraoperative and 27\% of the group had dislocation is a failure unique to the dual-mobility could at the time of initial dislocation or during A retrospective of THAs revision, and 15 reported a higher dislocation rate in dual-mobility compared with conventional the dual-mobility 5 patients had an dislocation in the 2 in the revision, and 1 in the conversion after a and all 5 a revision surgical and patients did not have further of the high rate of dislocations, et al. recommended or in the and performing the Joint A study on patients found that the risk of a periprosthetic joint infection following primary THA did not over a 15-year to and discharge to were associated with an increased risk of periprosthetic joint the for the diagnosis of a periprosthetic joint infection remains after the most recent of the A Society recently a of infection and for of after surgical treatment of periprosthetic joint The has been into of different of a or perceived or control without could be as infection control with as for reoperation and/or revision and/or as and as The the in 3 failure due to and failure due to the periprosthetic joint A high of was reported in a prospective cohort of patients with acute or periprosthetic joint infection patients, with patients with Sample is another with there is of in the time to and According to a study at the one-half of th